WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.150
Reservation-era modernity.

2
00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:05.190
This is where we restore
the world Curtis erased.

3
00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:08.340
By the 1890s and early 1900s,

4
00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:12.370
Indigenous nations were forming
political organizations,

5
00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:17.390
publishing newspapers,
fighting court cases over land and sovereignty,

6
00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:19.610
creating new art forms,

7
00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.650
blending traditional governance
with new strategies,

8
00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:27.850
wearing both traditional and modern clothing,
shaping pan-tribal movements.

9
00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:31.610
Curtis's world is not a record
of Indigenous society.

10
00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.790
It is a record
of his own nostalgic imagination.

11
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:41.470
Reservation-era life was complex,
creative, adaptive, and fiercely modern.
